# Accordion

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

An accordion hides optional content behind a control that opens and closes it. Use it for an extra explanation that only some people need: an uncommon term, a longer help topic or a set of troubleshooting steps. This page owns every show-and-hide disclosure in Civica, from one small definition to a group of sections.

Choose in this order:

1. Ordinary visible headings and a short contents list.
2. One native disclosure for an optional definition or short explanation.
3. Several independent disclosures for substantial help that a broad audience may need.
4. A grouped accordion with a show-all control, only when people need to compare independently opened topics.

This page covers:

- Accordion
  - `<details>`
    - `<summary>`
  - `ha-expansion-panel`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A disclosure has a label that is always visible and content that opens below it. The label is the only part that people can operate.

- In HTML, `details` is the container and `summary` is its label. The browser adds the disclosure marker, keyboard behaviour and the expanded or collapsed state.
- In Home Assistant, `ha-expansion-panel` has a header, an optional secondary line, a chevron and the content. Its content is drawn only while the panel is open.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-details"><code>&lt;details&gt;</code></h3>

The container for one disclosure.

- `open` shows the content when the page loads. Use it only when most people need the content.
- `name` puts several `details` in one group, so opening one closes the others. Use the same `name` for sections that people compare, and leave it out when people may want several open.
- The `toggle` event fires when the state changes.

The Civica theme has no keys for the native disclosure. It follows the surrounding text colour and font.

<h3 id="child-details--summary"><code>&lt;summary&gt;</code></h3>

The visible label, and it must be the first child of `details`. Enter or Space opens and closes it. It can hold text and a heading, but must not hold a link, a button or another control. Write what is hidden: “Why is this reading missing?”, not “More”.

<h3 id="child-ha-expansion-panel"><code>ha-expansion-panel</code></h3>

The native Home Assistant disclosure, used in settings pages and some cards.

- Attributes: `expanded` (open), `outlined` (draws a border), `left-chevron` (chevron before the header), `no-collapse` (cannot be closed), `header` and `secondary` (text).
- Slots: the default slot for content, plus `header`, `secondary`, `leading-icon`, `event` and `icons`.
- Events: `expanded-will-change` and `expanded-changed`.
- It opens with Enter or Space, and its summary is exposed as a button with its expanded state. The summary is at least 48 pixels tall.

Variables in the Civica theme that it reads: `outline-color`, `input-fill-color`, `primary-text-color`, `secondary-text-color`, `ha-card-border-radius` (square), `ha-font-size-s`, `ha-font-weight-medium` and `ha-color-fill-neutral-normal-active`.

The padding variables `expansion-panel-summary-padding` and `expansion-panel-content-padding` exist in Home Assistant, but the Civica theme does not set them.

Home Assistant removes the outline on the summary, so the default focus cue is a change of background and a circle behind the chevron. To draw the Civica focus outline you need the card-mod integration:

```yaml
card_mod:
  style:
    ha-expansion-panel$: |
      #summary:focus-visible {
        outline: 3px solid var(--civica-focus-text);
        outline-offset: -3px;
      }
```

Check the result in your own dashboard, in light and dark modes.

Home Assistant does not provide an exclusive-open group or a show-all control for its panel, and the theme cannot add them. Use native `details` with a shared `name` when you need a group.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Native `details` works without scripts, exposes its state to assistive technology and needs no integration.
- `ha-expansion-panel` matches the rest of the Home Assistant interface.
- Hidden content is harder to find, and people may never open it.
- The Home Assistant panel animates its height over 300 ms and has no reduced-motion rule.
- If a card is rebuilt whenever a sensor value updates, an open section may close. Keep the disclosure out of the part that updates.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use a disclosure for optional help. Keep the current state and the next required action outside it. Do not hide:

- a warning (see [Warning callout](warning-callout.html)),
- an error or a failed save,
- a required instruction or question,
- the current state of a device (see [Status](status.html)),
- anything most people need.

Do not place a disclosure around live device controls, and do not nest one disclosure inside another. Each native disclosure keeps its own open state. Do not copy a grouped accordion's state handling into one, and do not add a second script that tracks which sections are open. For a decision that needs focus, use a [dialog](dialogs.html).

In Home Assistant, an Energy view can keep a definition near a measurement, but the value and unit stay visible. A device card can explain why an action needs permission, but the reason it is unavailable stays visible too.

Opening or closing must not run a device command, reload data or discard a form people have started. Use the same labels in search results and related links.

Success criteria that apply:

- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value and 2.1.1 Keyboard: the label is a control with an exposed state.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible, 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum) and 2.4.13 Focus Appearance (AAA): the focus cue must be visible. Measure it before you claim it.
- 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast: the chevron and border need 3:1.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the label area is at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: it works at 320 CSS pixels.
- 3.2.2 On Input: opening does not change context.
- 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide and 2.3.3 Animation from Interactions (AAA): keep any motion short.

Hidden content raises the effort of finding it. Short, specific labels and no nesting keep that effort low.

